## The following text has been annotated for parts of speech by students at NTU Singapore as part of a class project in AY 2025-26.
## Verses are extracted from the taggedPBC. Annotations have been developed with a combination of manual and automated approaches, with reference to existing literature and (where possible) native speakers.
## ISO 639-3: bzj, Language: Belize Kriol English, Number of verses: 21
## References: Bembridge, N., Crosbie, C., Crosbie, P., Frank, D., Herrera, Y., & Rodriguez, A. (2024). Kriol-Inglish dikshineri: English-Kriol dictionary (2nd ed.). National Kriol Council. ; Escure, G. (1998). Gender and tense/aspect in Belizean creole. In J. Tent & F. Mugler (Eds.), SICOL: Proceedings of the Second International Conference on Oceanic Linguistics (pp. 27–41). Pacific Linguistics. Available at https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/server/api/core/bitstreams/6e91d862-f734-4b19-a78a-7dc49345e542/content ; Gómez Menjívar, J., & Salmon, W. (2018). Tropical tongues: Language and identity in Belize. University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill. ; Salmon, W. (2015). Language ideology, gender, and varieties of Belizean Kriol. Journal of Black Studies, 46(6), 605–625. http://www.jstor.org/stable/24572901 ; Schneider, E. W. (2017). English around the world: An introduction. Cambridge University Press.
